Breaking even typically requires 2 winners at odds of 3/1 or longer, covering the 46-bet stake through doubles and Single Stakes About returns. Three winning selections at similar odds generally produce profit through treble combinations and additional doubles. Shorter-priced favorites demand 4 or more winners to exceed the initial outlay, while longer odds reduce the winner threshold. The accumulator component delivers maximum returns when all 5 selections win, though Flag bet structures provide partial returns from fewer winners compared to standard accumulators.
## Frequently Asked Questions
### What happens if only two selections win in a Super Flag bet?
Two winning selections from five trigger one successful double bet from the 10 doubles in the Super Flag structure, plus the relevant Single Stakes About combination. The return depends entirely on the odds of those two winners — longer prices may cover the 46-bet stake, while short-priced favorites typically will not. Most Super Flag scenarios require three winners at moderate odds to generate profit, though two winners at 4/1 or longer can break even or produce small returns through the double and SSA components.
### Can I place a Super Flag on horses from the same race meeting?
Yes, all five selections can come from different races at the same meeting, which is common practice in horse racing. Bettors often select horses across consecutive races at venues like Cheltenham or Ascot to build a Super Flag. The calculator processes selections regardless of race timing or venue. However, some bookmakers may restrict certain combinations if races overlap, and dead-heat rules apply if multiple horses tie in any single race, affecting return calculations across the 46 bets.
### How does the Single Stakes About component differ from a regular double?
A Single Stakes About bet uses conditional staking — if the first selection wins, its returns automatically stake the second selection, whereas a regular double requires both selections to win for any return. The Super Flag includes 10 SSA bets pairing each selection with every other twice, once in each direction. This means if only one selection from a pair wins, the SSA still delivers a return through the conditional stake, providing insurance that standard doubles lack. The calculator accounts for these SSA returns separately from the 10 regular doubles.
### What minimum odds should selections have for a Super Flag to be worthwhile?
Selections averaging 2/1 to 4/1 (3.00 to 5.00 decimal) typically provide optimal value for Super Flag bets, balancing realistic win probability against sufficient return potential. Shorter odds below 6/4 often require four or five winners to exceed the 46-bet stake, while odds above 6/1 increase risk of multiple losers. The structure works best when at least three selections carry odds of 2/1 or longer, ensuring treble combinations generate meaningful profit if those three win. Mixing one or two shorter-priced selections with longer prices creates balanced coverage.
### Why does a Super Flag cost 46 bets instead of fewer like other accumulators?
The 46-bet total stems from including 10 Single Stakes About bets alongside standard accumulator combinations — this adds 15 more bets than a Lucky 31 covering the same five selections. While this increases the stake requirement, it provides better insurance when some selections lose, as the SSA component delivers returns that standard accumulators void entirely. The trade-off is clear: higher initial cost in exchange for partial-success coverage. Bettors seeking lower stakes should consider a Lucky 31 or straight fivefold accumulator instead.
### Do I need all five selections to win for any return from a Super Flag?
No — the Super Flag structure guarantees returns with just two winners from five through the successful double and Single Stakes About bets. Three winners activate multiple doubles and one treble, typically generating profit at moderate odds. Four winners trigger numerous trebles and fourfolds, while all five winning produces maximum returns across every bet type including the fivefold accumulator. This partial-success coverage distinguishes Flag bets from standard accumulators requiring all selections to win, though higher winner counts remain necessary for meaningful profit at shorter odds.
### Can I mix fractional and decimal odds in the same Super Flag calculation?
Yes, the Super Flag Calculator accepts both fractional odds like 5/2 or 7/4 and decimal odds like 3.50 or 2.25 within the same five selections. The calculator converts all odds to a common format internally before computing the 46 individual bet returns. Most GB bookmakers display odds in fractional format for horse racing and decimal for football, so mixed entry accommodates real betting scenarios where selections span multiple sports. The final return calculation remains identical regardless of input format.
### How do calculator projections compare to actual bookmaker payouts for Super Flags?
Calculator projections match actual bookmaker payouts exactly when using identical odds, as the mathematical combinations remain constant across operators. Discrepancies arise only if bookmakers apply different Each Way place terms, dead-heat reductions, or non-runner deductions that the calculator cannot anticipate without those specific inputs. Standard win-only Super Flags produce precise return predictions. Bettors should verify their bookmaker's Each Way terms and rule 4 deduction policies before placement, then input those exact parameters into the calculator for accurate Each Way projections.